            <ArticleTitle>The 148 M allele of the PNPLA3 is associated with plasma irisin levels in a population sample of Caucasian children: The PANIC Study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>793-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.metabol.2015.01.010</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0026-0495(15)00029-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">There are no previous data on the association of PNPLA3 I148M polymorphism (rs738409) with circulating adipokines and myokines in children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS/METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Subjects were a population sample of 481 Caucasian children aged 6-8 years. We assessed circulating levels of irisin together with IL-6, TNF-α, leptin, high molecular weight (HMW)-adiponectin, alanine aminotransferase (ALT) and gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT) while the subjects were stratified according to PNPLA3 I148M variants.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The PNPLA3 rs738409 polymorphism had a linear relationship with plasma levels of irisin after adjustment for age, sex and body height (p=0.007) but it was not associated with circulating levels of interleukin- 6 (IL-6), tumor-necrosis factor α (TNF-α), leptin or HMW-adiponectin.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">PNPLA3 148M allele carriers had higher plasma levels of irisin than the non-carriers. This might be due to compensatory mechanism to limit early steatotic and inflammatory changes in the liver.</AbstractText>
